  "text": "Certainly! Below is a Python function `word_histogram` that meets the requirements of your task. It:\n\n- Converts the input string to **lowercase** to ensure **case-insensitivity**.\n- Removes **punctuation**.\n- Splits the string into words.\n- Counts the frequency of each word using a dictionary.\n\n### \u2705 Implementation:\n\n```python\nimport string\n\ndef word_histogram(text):\n    # Convert to lowercase\n    text = text.lower()\n    \n    # Remove punctuation\n    text = text.translate(str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation))\n    \n    # Split into words\n    words = text.split()\n    \n    # Count word frequencies\n    histogram = {}\n    for word in words:\n        histogram[word] = histogram.get(word, 0) + 1\n    \n    return histogram\n```\n\n### \u2705 Unit Tests (as shown in your example):\n\n```python\nassert word_histogram(\"Hello, world! Hello.\") == {\"hello\": 2, \"world\": 1}\nassert word_histogram(\"Python is great. Python is fun!\") == {\"python\": 2, \"is\": 2, \"great\": 1, \"fun\": 1}\nassert word_histogram(\"Test, test, TEST.\") == {\"test\": 3}\n```\n\n### \u2705 Explanation:\n- **Lowercasing** ensures \"Hello\" and \"hello\" are treated as the same word.\n- **Removing punctuation** ensures words like \"world!\" are treated as \"world\".\n- **Splitting** by whitespace separates the words.\n- **Counting** with `get(..., 0)` handles the frequency logic.\n- The returned dictionary is consistent with your expected test outputs.\n\nLet me know if you'd like to extend this to handle other characters or edge cases!",
